《張釋之馮唐列傳》
Zhang Tingwei Zhi was from Duyang, named Ji. He had an elder brother Zhong living with him. He served as a cavalry attendant, attending Emperor Xiaowen, but for ten years received no promotion and was unknown. Zhi said: "Long neglect has reduced Zhong's property; I cannot continue." He wished to resign and return home. Vice General Yuan Yan knew his worth and regretted his departure, so he requested that Zhi be transferred to serve as an attendant. After Zhi had finished his audience, he raised the matter he had previously mentioned. Emperor Xiaowen said: "Do not be too high in your arguments; let it be implemented now." Thereupon Zhi spoke of the idle matters of Qin and Han, why Qin fell and Han prospered, long ago. Emperor Xiaowen praised him and appointed Zhi as an attendant and archer.

Zhi accompanied him and ascended the Tiger Circle. The Emperor asked the Shanglin Guard about the list of birds and beasts, asking more than ten questions. The guards looked left and right and could not answer. The Tiger Circle attendant, from the side, answered the Emperor's questions about the birds and beasts very thoroughly, wishing to observe his ability to respond fluently. Emperor Xiaowen said: "Should officials be like this? The guard is at fault!" He then ordered Zhi to be appointed as the Shanglin Commandant. Zhi had long previously said: "Your Majesty, what kind of person is Marquis Li Bo?" The Emperor said: "A capable person." He again asked: "What kind of person is Duke of Dongyang Zhang Xiang?" The Emperor again said: "A capable person." Zhi said: "If Marquis Li Bo and Duke of Dongyang are called capable, these two men could not even speak of matters, how could they compare to this attendant who speaks fluently and gives quick answers! Moreover, Qin employed officials of brush and ink; officials competed to be swift and meticulous in ranking, but their substance was only in documents, lacking hidden reality. Therefore their faults were not heard of, and they lingered until two generations, when the world collapsed. Now Your Majesty promotes this attendant based on his eloquence; I fear the world will follow the wind, competing for eloquence without substance. Furthermore, if the lower officials imitate the superiors in seeking glory, such mistakes must be examined." Emperor Xiaowen said: "Good." He stopped the appointment.

The Emperor got into his carriage and summoned Zhi to ride with him, moving slowly, and asked Zhi about the faults of Qin. Zhi answered with substance. Upon reaching the palace, the Emperor appointed Zhi as the Director of the Imperial Chariots.

Later, the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang rode together into court, not dismounting at the Siming Gate. Zhi stopped the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang, not allowing them to enter the palace gate. He then accused them of not dismounting at the public gate as disrespectful and submitted a memorial. Empress Dowager Bo heard of this, and Emperor Xiaowen removed his cap to apologize, saying: "I have failed to teach my children properly." Empress Dowager Bo then sent an envoy with an imperial edict to pardon the Crown Prince and the Prince of Liang, after which they were allowed to enter. Emperor Xiaowen thus admired Zhi and appointed him as a Grand Counselor.

Later, he became a Vice General. Accompanying him to the Mausoleum, he stood north of the mourning hall. At that time, Consort Yan accompanied him. The Emperor pointed out the new road at Xinfeng to Consort Yan, saying: "This is the road to Yongjia." He ordered Consort Yan to beat the drum, and the Emperor himself leaned on the drum and sang, feeling sorrowful and melancholy. He then said to the ministers: "Alas! Using stones from the northern mountain as pillars, using silk cushions to line the tomb, and coating the doors with lacquer—can this move the spirits?" Those on the left and right all said: "Good." Zhi stepped forward and said: "If there is something desirable within, even if you carve the southern mountain, there would be regret; if there is nothing desirable within, even without stone pillars, what sorrow would there be!" Emperor Xiaowen praised him. Later, he appointed Zhi as the Chief Justice.

Later, the Emperor went out to the Zhongyu Bridge. A person ran out from under the bridge, and the carriage horse was startled. The Emperor ordered the cavalry to capture him and handed him to the Chief Justice. Zhi interrogated him. The man said: "A county man came, heard the noise, and hid under the bridge. After a long time, thinking the procession had passed, he came out, saw the imperial carriage and cavalry, and ran away." The Chief Justice Qin Dang sentenced one person who had committed a crime to a fine. Emperor Xiaowen was angry, saying: "This person startled my horse; my horse is gentle and mild; even if he rode another horse, would it not injure me? Yet the Chief Justice sentences him to a fine!" Zhi said: "The law is what the Son of Heaven shares with the world. Now the law is like this, yet you make it heavier; this is a law not trusted by the people. Moreover, at that time, if Your Majesty had ordered his execution, it would have been done. Now that you have handed him to the Chief Justice, the Chief Justice is the balance of the world. If it tilts, the application of law throughout the world will become light or heavy; where will the people place their hands and feet? I hope Your Majesty examines this." After a while, the Emperor said: "The Chief Justice is correct."

Later, someone stole the jade ring in front of the Gao Temple, was captured, and Emperor Xiaowen was angry, handing him to the Chief Justice for judgment. Zhi, according to the law, sentenced a thief of temple property and sacrificial vessels to be exposed in the marketplace. Emperor Xiaowen was greatly angered, saying: "People's waywardness leads them to steal the vessels of the ancestral temple. I hand him to the Chief Justice, wishing to execute his clan, yet you sentence him according to the law; this is not my intention in inheriting the ancestral temple." Zhi removed his cap, prostrated, and apologized, saying: "The law is sufficient. Moreover, crimes are graded, but there is a difference between rebellion and obedience. Now stealing temple vessels and executing the clan is but one in ten thousand. Suppose a foolish man takes a handful of earth from the Changling Mausoleum; how would Your Majesty add to his law?" After a while, Emperor Xiaowen and Empress Dowager Bo discussed it and allowed the Chief Justice to sentence him. At that time, the Minister of War, Marquis Zhou Yafu, and the Duke of Liangxiang, Wang Hun, seeing Zhi uphold fair discussion, became friends with him. Zhang Tingwei was thus renowned throughout the world.

After Emperor Xiaowen died, Emperor Jing ascended the throne. Zhi feared and feigned illness. He wished to resign, fearing a great execution; he wished to apologize, but did not know how. Following Wang Sheng's plan, he finally apologized, and Emperor Jing merely tolerated it.

Wang Sheng was a man skilled in the words of Huang and Lao, a recluse. He had been summoned to reside in the court. When the Three Dukes and Nine Ministers all gathered and stood, Wang Sheng, an old man, said: "I can untie the knot." He said to Zhang Tingwei: "Tie the knot for me!" Zhi knelt and tied it. Afterward, someone asked Wang Sheng: "Why do you alone humiliate Zhang Tingwei, making him kneel and tie the knot?" Wang Sheng said: "I am old and useless, knowing I will be of no benefit to Zhang Tingwei. Zhang Tingwei is now a famous minister of the world; I therefore jokingly humiliate the Chief Justice, making him kneel and tie the knot, to emphasize him." The ministers heard this, admired Wang Sheng, and respected Zhang Tingwei.

Zhang Tingwei served Emperor Jing for over a year as the Prime Minister of Huainan, still adhering to his previous conduct. Later, Zhi died. His son was Zhang Zhi, named Changgong, who became a Grand Counselor but was dismissed. Because he could not conform to the manners of the age, he never held office again.

Feng Tang's great-grandfather was from Zhao. His father was exiled. When Han was established, they settled in Anling. Tang was filial and became the Chief Attendant of the Central Court, serving Emperor Xiaowen. Emperor Xiaowen traveled and asked Tang: "Old man, how did you become an attendant? Where is your home?" Tang truthfully answered. Emperor Xiaowen said: "When I was in Dai, I still ate the food sent by General Gao Bi, who told me of the virtue of Zhao General Li Qi, who fought at Qilianxia. Now whenever I eat, I never fail to think of Qilianxia. Do you know this?" Tang replied: "Not as good as Lian Po and Li Mu as generals." The Emperor asked: "Why?" Tang said: "My great-grandfather, when in Zhao, served as an official (died) [commander] general, and was good to Li Mu. My father was a former prime minister and was good to Zhao General Li Qi, knowing his character." The Emperor, having heard of the character of Lian Po and Li Mu, spoke well of them, and beating his chest, said: "Alas! If only I had Lian Po and Li Mu as my generals, would I fear the Huns?" Tang said: "Your Majesty! Even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you could not use them." The Emperor was angry, stood up, and entered the inner chambers. After a while, he summoned Tang and rebuked him: "Why do you humiliate me before the crowd, with no leisure at all?" Tang apologized: "I am a rustic and do not know the taboo."

At that time, the Huns newly entered the court at Na, killing the Northern Territory Commander Ji. The Emperor suspected Hu Rong, and after a while, asked Tang again: "How do you know I cannot use Lian Po and Li Mu?" Tang replied: "I have heard that in ancient times, when kings sent generals, they knelt and pushed the cart, saying: 'Within the cart, I control; outside the cart, the general controls.' Military merit and rewards are all decided outside, and reported upon return. This is not empty words. My great-grandfather said that Li Mu, as a general of Zhao on the frontier, used the market taxes of the army to feed the soldiers, and rewards were decided outside, not interfered with from within. Entrusted and responsible for success, Li Mu was able to exhaust his wisdom and ability, selecting one thousand three hundred chariots, ten thousand cavalry, and one hundred thousand men of a thousand gold, thus driving north to defeat the Xiongnu, destroy the Eastern Hu, eliminate the Zanglin, restrain the strong Qin in the west, and support Han and Wei in the south. At that time, Zhao was nearly hegemonic. Later, when Zhao Wang moved and established the capital, his mother was the regent. Wang established the capital and used Guo Kai's slander, finally killing Li Mu and appointing Yan Ju as his successor. Thus the army was defeated and the soldiers perished, being consumed by Qin. Now I have heard that Wei is still the commander of Yunzhong, and its army market taxes are all used to feed the soldiers, with private funds for nourishment, slaughtering a cow every five days to entertain guests, military officers, and attendants. Thus the Huns keep far away and do not approach the Yunzhong border. When the Huns once invaded, they still led chariots and cavalry to attack them, killing many of their men. The soldiers are all sons of commoners, conscripted from the fields; who knows if they are worth a measure of grain? They fight all day, cut off heads and capture prisoners, but their merits are not recorded, and a single word of praise is not answered. Civil officials restrain them with the law. Rewards are not given, yet officials enforce the law and must punish. I, in my foolishness, think Your Majesty's law is too clear, rewards too light, and punishments too heavy. Moreover, the Yunzhong commander Wei Shang, for his merits in killing Huns, was promoted six levels; Your Majesty handed him to the officials, stripped his rank, and punished him. From this, I say: Even if Your Majesty obtained Lian Po and Li Mu, you could not use them. I am truly foolish, touching the taboo, deserving death, deserving death!" Emperor Xiaowen was pleased. That day he ordered Feng Tang to hold the seal and pardon Wei Shang, restoring him as the commander of Yunzhong, and appointed Tang as the Commander of Chariots and Cavalry, in charge of the Minister of War and the chariot soldiers of the commanderies and states.

Seven years later, Emperor Jing ascended the throne and appointed Tang as the Prime Minister of Chu, but he was dismissed. When Emperor Wu ascended the throne, seeking the virtuous and capable, he recommended Feng Tang. Tang was then over ninety years old and could no longer serve as an official, so he appointed Tang's son Feng Sui as an attendant. Sui was named Wangsun, also an extraordinary man, and was friends with Yu Shan.

The Grand Historian said: Zhang Ji's words about "capable persons" upheld the law without flattering intentions; Feng Gong's discussion of generals and commanders was truly meaningful! Truly meaningful! The saying goes: "If you do not know the person, look at his friends." The praise of these two gentlemen can be inscribed on the corridors and halls. The Book says: "Not partial, not factional, the royal way is vast and expansive; not factional, not partial, the royal way is convenient and easy." Zhang Ji and Feng Gong approached this. [end of text]


